@KoBeWi This proposal does not create two-way communication. If anything, adding the proposed signals to the body *avoids* unnecessary responsibility. The function that emits the signal on the Area node already has a reference to the body, so there is no additional responsibility. However, if the signal on the area had to be picked up by a user's GDScript so another can be emitted on the body, that adds (slight) communication complexity on the project side.

It seems like a sensible idea to allow bodies to have their logic self-contained if the user chooses to do this. The question is how common this is, because it can be worked around easily. One thing I am still a bit confused by is why @Illauriel iterated on their workaround by placing an Area on the character node. That seems much worse than this:

```gdscript
extends Area3D

func _on_body_entered(body: PhysicsBody3D) -> void:
	if body.has_method(&"start_swimming"):
		body.start_swimming()

func _on_body_exited(body: PhysicsBody3D) -> void:
	if body.has_method(&"stop_swimming"):
		body.stop_swimming() # This may need to be more complicated to handle adjacent areas but still that complexity would also exist with signals on the body.
```

> these properties are built into WaterArea custom node, and I don't have scripts attached to it^^'

The same principle for the workaround applies in the C++ code for your WaterArea:

```cpp
WaterArea::WaterArea() {
	connect("body_entered", callable_mp(this, &WaterArea::_on_body_entered));
	connect("body_exited", callable_mp(this, &WaterArea::_on_body_exited));
}

void WaterArea::_on_body_entered(Node *body) {
	if (body->has_method("start_swimming")) {
		body->call("start_swimming");
	}
}

void WaterArea::_on_body_exited(Node *body) {
	if (body->has_method("stop_swimming")) {
		body->call("stop_swimming");
	}
}
```

@sammonius Triangle strips will only be more efficient than indexed triangle lists in very rare cases. The trouble with triangle strips is that they only work for geometry that can be well represented by a row of triangles where each triangle shares an edge with the next triangle. Modern meshes used in games often require more complex representation and may even have floating triangles not connected to others. 

On modern hardware, indexed vertices are cached, so there is no performance benefit to using triangle strips at render time. 

This stack overflow answer includes some nice analysis on the subject. https://stackoverflow.com/a/17926147
